// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// womb/kernel/trap/trap.vuma
// VWK Trap Dispatcher — routes traps by vector number (PMT-pure).
// Wave R2f — migrated onto the canonical superset TrapFrame (R2a).
// Wave W13S1 — de-stubbed trap_panic / trap_syscall / trap_irq.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// This file is the arch-independent trap dispatcher. After trap_entry (R2b/
// R2c/R2d's per-arch trap_trampoline.vuma) saves the native save area and
// the per-arch trap_to_superset adapter translates it into the canonical
// superset TrapFrame (R2a's womb/kernel/trap/trap_frame.vuma — 576 bytes,
// 8 fields), control reaches trap_handler(tf, ...) here. The dispatcher
// routes by `tf.vector` (the interrupt-vector / exception-cause field):
//
//     vector 0..31   → CPU exception  → trap_panic(tf)
//                                       (W13S1: prints "*** KERNEL PANIC
//                                        ***\nvector=N pc=N\n" via write(1,
//                                        string-literal, ...) + exit(1).
//                                        Previously a bare `return;` stub.)
//     vector 128     → syscall (int 0x80 on x86_64, SVC #0 on aarch64,
//                       ECALL on riscv64 — the per-arch adapter normalizes
//                       all three into vector == 128 here) → trap_syscall(tf,
//                       syscall_tbl) (W13S1: calls syscall_dispatch_from_trap
//                       re-declared byte-identically to dispatch.vuma R4c.
//                       Previously a bare `return;` stub.)
//     everything else → IRQ → trap_irq(tf, irq_ring, irq_tbl)
//                                       (W13S1: pushes tf.vector onto the
//                                        IRQ ring via irq_ring_push, then
//                                        drains via irq_dispatch_loop — both
//                                        re-declared byte-identically to
//                                        irq.vuma R2f. Previously a bare
//                                        `return;` stub.)
//
// The dispatcher uses ONLY superset TrapFrame fields (tf.vector in the
// router; tf.pc / tf.sp / tf.error_code / tf.status / tf.gpr / tf.fpu are
// available for the per-handler consumers trap_panic / trap_syscall /
// trap_irq to read but are NOT touched by the router itself). No arch-
// specific field names like `tf.rax` (x86_64) or `tf.x0` (aarch64) or
// `tf.ra` (riscv64) appear anywhere in this file — the dispatcher is
// genuinely arch-independent, the R2 Golden Rule in TASKS.md ("Do not
// write arch-conditional logic inside kernel transforms").
//
// ── Why is the layout re-declared byte-identically to R2a? ──────────────────
// VUMA has no `import` statement (Open Work §7), so this file re-declares
// the canonical superset TrapFrame + tf_get_gpr + tf_set_gpr + tf_init
// byte-identically to womb/kernel/trap/trap_frame.vuma (R2a). The same
// byte-identical-re-declaration invariant that R2b/R2c/R2d's per-arch
// trap_trampoline.vuma files follow extends here: a mismatch in field
// order or type would silently produce different field offsets at the
// call site, so the layout + 3 helpers are copied verbatim from R2a's
// canonical file.
//
// ── W13S1: trap_panic de-stubbing ───────────────────────────────────────────
// trap_panic is the CPU-exception path. W13S1 replaces the bare `return;`
// stub with a real panic print:
//   1. Read tf.vector + tf.pc into LOCAL u64's BEFORE the first extern call
//      (the codegen conservatively invalidates State<T> on extern calls —
//      caching the values into locals first means the extern-call invalida-
//      tion of `tf` is harmless, same pattern as dispatch.vuma's
//      `let nr = tf_get_gpr(tf, 0);` before `__call_indirect1(handler, tf
//      as Address)`).
//   2. write(1, "*** KERNEL PANIC ***\n", 21) — Wave 1 string literal lowers
//      to a .rodata address; `write` takes it directly as `buf: Address`.
//      Same PMT "Borrowed read" pattern as shell.vuma's `write(1, "vwk: ", 5)`.
//   3. write(1, "vector=", 7) + trap_put_dec(buf, vec) + write(1, buf as
//      Address, n) — decimal expansion of the vector number into a local
//      State<PanicBuf> (32-byte data array), then write the digit bytes.
//   4. write(1, " pc=", 4) + trap_put_dec(buf, pc) + write(1, buf as
//      Address, n) — decimal expansion of the PC. The buf State is reused
//      (trap_put_dec overwrites buf.data[0..n] — a fresh write, safe after
//      the prior extern call per dispatch.vuma's post-call write pattern).
//   5. write(1, "\n", 1) — trailing newline.
//   6. exit(1) — halt. Does not return; `return;` below is unreachable but
//      satisfies VUMA's parser.
//
// The self-test does NOT exercise trap_panic (vector=0) because exit(1)
// would abort the test process before the remaining checks run. Same
// pattern as panic.vuma's self-test (assert(1, msg) — the pass path only).
// The IVE verifier still statically checks trap_panic's body.
//
// ── W13S1: trap_syscall de-stubbing ─────────────────────────────────────────
// trap_syscall now calls syscall_dispatch_from_trap(tbl, tf) — re-declared
// byte-identically to womb/kernel/syscall/dispatch.vuma (R4c). The
// SyscallTable layout + syscall_table_get helper + the dispatch function
// + the __call_indirect1 extern are all re-declared here (no import). The
// dispatch reads nr from canonical GPR slot 0, looks up the handler,
// invokes it via __call_indirect1 if registered (writes result to slot 7),
// or writes 38 (-ENOSYS) to slot 7 if out-of-range / unregistered. In the
// self-test the table is empty, so dispatch writes 38 to slot 7 and
// returns 38 — no crash.
//
// ── W13S1: trap_irq de-stubbing ────────────────────────────────────────────
// trap_irq now:
//   1. irq_ring_push(ring, tf.vector) — enqueues the faulting vector onto
//      the per-CPU IrqRing (re-declared byte-identically to irq.vuma R2f).
//   2. irq_dispatch_loop(ring, tbl) — drains the ring: pops each vector,
//      looks up the handler in the IrqTable, invokes it via
//      __call_indirect1 if registered, sends EOI via pic_eoi. In the self-
//      test the table is empty, so the loop pops the pushed vector, finds
//      handler == 0 (spurious), skips EOI, pops the 9999 empty sentinel,
//      and returns — no crash.
// The IrqRing + IrqTable layouts + irq_ring_push + irq_ring_pop +
// irq_get_handler + irq_dispatch_loop + the pic_eoi / __call_indirect1
// externs are all re-declared here (no import).
//
// ── Why nested ifs instead of else-if? ──────────────────────────────────────
// VUMA's if-expression syntax (K2a) supports `if cond { ... } else { ... }`
// but not `else if` chained. The dispatcher therefore uses nested ifs:
//     if v < 32 { panic } else { if v == 128 { syscall } else { irq } }
// This is the same nested-if pattern K3c's trap_is_page_fault uses for
// its range check (12 <= scause <= 15 → nested-if form).
//
// ── PMT discipline ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NO `*ptr`, `&x`, `allocate`, or `free` anywhere in this file. All layouts
// (TrapFrame, PanicBuf, SyscallTable, IrqRing, IrqTable) are flat typed-
// memory records held in `___pmt_buffer`-backed State<T>'s; the CALLER
// allocates them via `state_new(T)` and passes them by reference to
// trap_handler / tf_init / etc. (init-style API). trap_panic allocates
// its own local State<PanicBuf> via state_new(PanicBuf) — same pattern as
// panic.vuma's `let banner = state_new(PanicMsg)`.
//
// The `buf as Address` / `tf as Address` casts are pure arena-offset
// extractions (no pointer arithmetic, no heap allocation) — the same PMT
// pattern used by console_flush (`c as Address`), hw_aes_encrypt (`key as
// Address`), and dispatch.vuma's `__call_indirect1(handler, tf as Address)`.
//
// The sub-handlers (trap_panic, trap_syscall, trap_irq) take State<TrapFrame>
// by regular VUMA-fn borrow (NOT an extern #[borrow] — VUMA fn calls don't
// need the attribute). trap_panic reads tf.vector / tf.pc into locals BEFORE
// the first extern write call (so the post-extern invalidation of tf is
// harmless — the locals hold the values). trap_syscall passes tf to
// syscall_dispatch_from_trap which internally passes `tf as Address` to
// __call_indirect1 (extern) — that invalidates tf, but the dispatch's only
// post-extern tf access is `tf_set_gpr(tf, 7, result)` (a fresh write, safe
// per dispatch.vuma's documented pattern). trap_irq reads tf.vector into
// the irq_ring_push call arg before any extern fires; irq_dispatch_loop's
// externs (__call_indirect1, pic_eoi) take only u64/u8 scalars, so neither
// IrqRing nor IrqTable is invalidated.
//
// ── VUMA has no `import` ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// This file is self-contained. It re-declares:
//   - TrapFrame + tf_get_gpr + tf_set_gpr + tf_init (byte-identical to R2a's
//     trap_frame.vuma — the dispatcher needs its own copy).
//   - SyscallTable + syscall_table_get + syscall_dispatch_from_trap (byte-
//     identical to R4c's dispatch.vuma — trap_syscall needs its own copy).
//   - IrqRing + IrqTable + irq_ring_push + irq_ring_pop + irq_get_handler +
//     irq_dispatch_loop (byte-identical to R2f's irq.vuma — trap_irq needs
//     its own copy).
//   - PanicBuf + trap_put_dec (new in W13S1 — the decimal-conversion helper
//     for trap_panic's vector/PC print).
// A mismatch in field order or type would silently produce different field
// offsets at the call site — the byte-identical-re-declaration invariant
// (K2c "extern signatures MUST stay byte-identical", extended to layouts
// in K3d) extends to all re-declared layouts + helpers here.
//
// ── Decimal-constants discipline ────────────────────────────────────────────
// All non-zero constants in this file use DECIMAL per the R2f contract's
// "Use decimal constants" rule. Decimal avoids the parser's hex-literal
// path (parse_int_radix width-extension subtleties — see
// womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for the same rationale, and
// R2a's trap_frame.vuma for the same rule applied to vector=14 and
// pc=4096). The constants:
//     32  = 0x20  boundary between CPU exceptions (0..31) and IRQs
//                 (32..N) — the 8259 PIC's IRQs are remapped to vectors
//                 32..47 by the IDT setup in K11's boot path on x86_64.
//     128 = 0x80  the int 0x80 syscall vector on x86_64 (Linux's legacy
//                 32-bit syscall ABI; the per-arch adapter normalizes
//                 aarch64 SVC #0 and riscv64 ECALL into vector == 128).
//     0   = self-test vector 0 (CPU exception — lowest valid exception
//                 vector on all arches).
//     256, 8, 24, 255, 0  = layout + helper constants (same as R2a).
//     21, 7, 4, 1 = string-literal byte counts for trap_panic's write
//                 calls (banner / "vector=" / " pc=" / "\n").
//     10, 48      = trap_put_dec constants (10 = decimal base, 48 = '0'
//                   ASCII origin for digit conversion).
//     512, 38, 7  = syscall_dispatch_from_trap constants (512 = max
//                   syscalls / bounds-check, 38 = ENOSYS, 7 = ret slot).
//     4096, 2048  = SyscallTable.handlers / IrqTable.handlers array sizes.
//     9999, 31    = IrqRing empty sentinel / 32-entry wrap mask.
//
// ── Build & verify (hosted x86_64) ──────────────────────────────────────────
//     . "$HOME/.cargo/env" && CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 \
//         cargo build --profile release-fast --bin compile_dump
//     ./target/release-fast/compile_dump \
//         womb/kernel/trap/trap.vuma \
//         /tmp/trap.bin x86_64 --verify
//     /tmp/trap.bin ; echo "exit=$?"
// Expected: IVE Pass, exit=0. The self-test exercises the syscall + IRQ
// paths (vector 128 → trap_syscall with an empty SyscallTable → dispatch
// writes 38 to slot 7; vector 32 → trap_irq pushes 32 onto the ring then
// drains it via irq_dispatch_loop). The panic path (vector 0) is NOT
// exercised by the self-test (trap_panic calls exit(1) which would abort
// the test before the remaining checks run — same pattern as panic.vuma's
// assert(1, msg) pass-path-only self-test).

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Extern "C" trampolines.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// W13S1: trap_panic needs write + exit (same declarations as panic.vuma /
// shell.vuma). trap_syscall's syscall_dispatch_from_trap needs
// __call_indirect1 (same as dispatch.vuma). trap_irq's irq_dispatch_loop
// needs __call_indirect1 + pic_eoi (same as irq.vuma). All re-declared
// byte-identically to the canonical extern blocks.
//
// Hosted mode: write → sys_write (nr 1), exit → sys_exit (nr 60) — both
// pre-registered on x86_64. __call_indirect1 → real asm stub
// (`mov rax,rdi; mov rdi,rsi; jmp rax`). pic_eoi → __ffi_fallback_stub
// (no-op). None emit a fallback warning.

extern "C" {
    // sys_write (nr 1): write `count` bytes from `buf` to fd `fd`. The
    // `buf: Address` (NOT State<T>) means the FFI marshal does NOT
    // invalidate any state on the call — the Address is just a raw u64
    // pointer handed to the syscall. Same declaration as panic.vuma:98,
    // shell.vuma:268.
    fn write(fd: i64, buf: Address, count: i64) -> i64;

    // sys_exit (nr 60): terminate the process with exit code `code`.
    // Does NOT return — the stub ends with INT3 as a safety guard. Same
    // declaration as panic.vuma:107.
    fn exit(code: i64);

    // Wave 5: indirect function call trampoline. Tail-calls the function
    // at `ptr` with `arg` in RDI (SysV AMD64). Used by
    // syscall_dispatch_from_trap (to invoke per-syscall handlers) and
    // irq_dispatch_loop (to invoke per-vector IRQ handlers). Same
    // declaration as dispatch.vuma:226, irq.vuma:276.
    fn __call_indirect1(ptr: u64, arg: u64) -> u64;

    // Send End-Of-Interrupt to the 8259 PIC. K11 stub: `mov al, 0x20;
    // out 0x20, al; ret`. Hosted: __ffi_fallback_stub (no-op). Same
    // declaration as irq.vuma:269.
    fn pic_eoi(irq: u8);
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Layouts.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Canonical superset TrapFrame (576 bytes total) — re-declared byte-
// identically to R2a's womb/kernel/trap/trap_frame.vuma. See R2a's header
// for the field semantics (gpr 32×u64 LE-packed in 256 bytes; pc/status/
// vector/error_code/sp are 5 scalar u64 fields; fpu is 32×u64 LE-packed
// in 256 bytes; _pad is 24 alignment bytes).
layout TrapFrame = {
    gpr: [u8; 256],        // 32 × 8 bytes — general-purpose registers (LE)
    pc: u64,               // program counter (rip / elr_el1 / mepc)
    status: u64,           // status register (rflags / spsr_el1 / mstatus)
    vector: u64,           // interrupt vector / exception cause
    error_code: u64,       // CPU error code / esr_el1 / stval
    sp: u64,               // stack pointer at trap entry
    fpu: [u8; 256],        // 32 × 8 bytes — FPU/SIMD registers (optional, LE)
    _pad: [u8; 24],        // alignment padding (zeroed by tf_init)
}

// W13S1: PanicBuf — 32-byte decimal digit buffer for trap_panic's vector
// and PC print. 32 bytes is enough for any u64 in decimal (max 20 digits
// for 18446744073709551615). The `data` field is at offset 0 so that
// `buf as Address` yields the address of data[0] — the natural argument
// for write(1, buf_as_addr, n). Same offset-0-buf pattern as panic.vuma's
// PanicMsg (panic.vuma:123) and shell.vuma's ShellOut (shell.vuma:276).
layout PanicBuf = {
    data: [u8; 32],   // decimal digit bytes (offset 0 — `as Address` yields &data[0])
    _pad: [u8; 4],    // alignment (zeroed by state_new)
}

// Re-declared byte-identically to R4c's dispatch.vuma (dispatch.vuma:252).
// 512 entries × 8 bytes per entry. count tracks registered handlers
// (incremented by syscall_register in dispatch.vuma; this file doesn't
// re-declare syscall_register because the self-test doesn't register any
// handlers — the empty-table path is what the self-test exercises).
layout SyscallTable = {
    handlers: [u8; 4096],  // 512 * 8 bytes — u64 handler addr per syscall
    count: u32,
}

// Re-declared byte-identically to R2f's irq.vuma (irq.vuma:224). 32-entry
// SPSC ring buffer for IRQ vectors. head = read pos, tail = write pos,
// count = entries currently in buffer (0..32).
layout IrqRing = {
    buf: [u8; 256],   // 32 × 8 bytes — u64 IRQ vectors (LE-packed)
    head: u32,        // read position (0..31, wraps via & 31)
    tail: u32,        // write position (0..31, wraps via & 31)
    count: u32,       // entries currently in buffer (0..32)
}

// Re-declared byte-identically to R2f's irq.vuma (irq.vuma:241). 256
// entries (one per interrupt vector), each a u64 handler address or 0.
layout IrqTable = {
    handlers: [u8; 2048],  // 256 * 8 bytes — u64 handler addr per IRQ
    count: u32,
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// TrapFrame helpers (re-declared byte-identically to R2a's trap_frame.vuma).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// tf_get_gpr / tf_set_gpr / tf_init — copied verbatim from R2a so this
// file can be compiled standalone (no `import`). The self-test calls
// tf_init to zero a fresh TrapFrame before exercising the dispatcher.

// Read the u64 GPR at canonical slot `idx` from tf.gpr. 8-byte LE unpack
// at byte offset `idx * 8`. Same loop pattern as task.vuma's pt_get_pid
// but with a u64 accumulator and 8-byte loop bound. No `*ptr` / `&tf` /
// `allocate` anywhere.
fn tf_get_gpr(tf: State<TrapFrame>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tf.gpr[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 `val` to canonical slot `idx` in tf.gpr. 8-byte LE pack
// at byte offset `idx * 8`. Same loop pattern as task.vuma's pt_set_pid
// but with 8-byte loop bound + u64 mask. No `*ptr` / `&tf` / `allocate`.
fn tf_set_gpr(tf: State<TrapFrame>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tf.gpr[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// Zero every field of a caller-allocated TrapFrame. Called once per
// TrapFrame before any arch-specific trap_to_superset adapter (R2b/R2c/
// R2d) populates the arch-specific fields. Explicit byte-zeroing loops
// for gpr/fpu/_pad (not relying on state_new's zero-fill) — same pattern
// as R1a's bootinfo_init. No `*ptr` / `&tf` / `allocate` anywhere.
fn tf_init(tf: State<TrapFrame>) {
    let i = 0;
    while i < 256 { tf.gpr[i] = 0; i = i + 1; }
    tf.pc = 0;
    tf.status = 0;
    tf.vector = 0;
    tf.error_code = 0;
    tf.sp = 0;
    let j = 0;
    while j < 256 { tf.fpu[j] = 0; j = j + 1; }
    let k = 0;
    while k < 24 { tf._pad[k] = 0; k = k + 1; }
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PanicBuf helper (W13S1).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// trap_put_dec: write the decimal expansion of `val` into buf.data[0..n]
// and return n (the digit count). For val == 0, writes a single '0' and
// returns 1. For val > 0, counts digits by repeated division by 10, then
// fills buf.data backwards from position n-1 down to 0 (so the digits
// end up in the correct left-to-right order). PMT-pure: no `*ptr`, no
// `&buf`, no `allocate` — just typed array writes via `buf.data[idx]`.
//
// The caller (trap_panic) passes `buf as Address` + the returned count to
// write(1, buf_as_addr, n). 32 bytes is enough for any u64 (max 20 digits).
fn trap_put_dec(buf: State<PanicBuf>, val: u64) -> u32 {
    // Special case: val == 0 → single '0' digit.
    if val == 0 {
        buf.data[0] = 48;  // '0'
        return 1;
    }
    // Count decimal digits by repeated division by 10.
    let n: u32 = 0;
    let v: u64 = val;
    while v > 0 {
        n = n + 1;
        v = v / 10;
    }
    // Fill buf.data backwards from position n-1 down to 0. After the
    // loop, buf.data[0..n] holds the decimal digits in left-to-right
    // order. (v2 is consumed from the low end via v2 % 10, so the first
    // digit extracted is the least-significant — placed at the highest
    // position, then we move leftward.)
    let v2: u64 = val;
    let pos: u32 = n;
    while v2 > 0 {
        pos = pos - 1;
        let d: u64 = v2 % 10;
        buf.data[pos] = (48 + d) as u8;
        v2 = v2 / 10;
    }
    return n;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// SyscallTable helpers + dispatch (re-declared byte-identically to R4c's
// dispatch.vuma).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// syscall_table_get + syscall_dispatch_from_trap — copied verbatim from
// dispatch.vuma so trap_syscall can call syscall_dispatch_from_trap without
// a cross-file import (VUMA has none). tf_get_gpr / tf_set_gpr are shared
// with this file's own TrapFrame helpers above (byte-identical).

// Read the u64 handler address for syscall `nr` from SyscallTable.handlers.
// Re-declared byte-identically to dispatch.vuma:321.
fn syscall_table_get(tbl: State<SyscallTable>, nr: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = nr * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.handlers[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Syscall dispatch from the superset TrapFrame. Re-declared byte-identically
// to dispatch.vuma:387. Reads nr from slot 0, bounds-checks (nr >= 512 →
// -ENOSYS), looks up handler, invokes via __call_indirect1 if registered,
// writes result to slot 7. The `tf as Address` cast on the indirect call
// invalidates tf (conservative codegen rule), but the post-call
// tf_set_gpr(tf, 7, result) is a fresh write — safe per dispatch.vuma's
// documented pattern.
fn syscall_dispatch_from_trap(tbl: State<SyscallTable>, tf: State<TrapFrame>) -> u64 {
    let nr = tf_get_gpr(tf, 0);
    if nr >= 512 {
        tf_set_gpr(tf, 7, 38);
        return 38;
    }
    let handler = syscall_table_get(tbl, nr as u32);
    if handler == 0 {
        tf_set_gpr(tf, 7, 38);
        return 38;
    }
    let result = __call_indirect1(handler, tf as Address) as u64;
    tf_set_gpr(tf, 7, result);
    return result;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// IrqRing + IrqTable helpers + dispatch_loop (re-declared byte-identically
// to R2f's irq.vuma).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// irq_ring_push + irq_ring_pop + irq_get_handler + irq_dispatch_loop —
// copied verbatim from irq.vuma so trap_irq can push the faulting vector
// and drain the ring without a cross-file import.

// Enqueue one IRQ vector into the ring. Producer side. Returns 0 on
// success, -1 (written as `0 - 1`) if the ring is full. Re-declared
// byte-identically to irq.vuma:308.
fn irq_ring_push(ring: State<IrqRing>, vector: u64) -> i64 {
    if ring.count >= 32 {
        return 0 - 1;  // -1 (full)
    }
    // Write vector at tail position (8-byte LE pack).
    let off = ring.tail * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (vector >> sh) & 255;
        ring.buf[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    ring.tail = (ring.tail + 1) & 31;  // wrap around 32
    ring.count = ring.count + 1;
    return 0;
}

// Dequeue one IRQ vector from the ring. Consumer side. Returns the u64
// vector, or 9999 (empty sentinel) if the ring is empty. Re-declared
// byte-identically to irq.vuma:333.
fn irq_ring_pop(ring: State<IrqRing>) -> u64 {
    if ring.count == 0 {
        return 9999;  // empty sentinel
    }
    // Read vector at head position (8-byte LE unpack).
    let off = ring.head * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = ring.buf[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    ring.head = (ring.head + 1) & 31;  // wrap around 32
    ring.count = ring.count - 1;
    return v;
}

// Read the u64 handler address for IRQ `irq` from IrqTable.handlers.
// Re-declared byte-identically to irq.vuma:366.
fn irq_get_handler(tbl: State<IrqTable>, irq: u8) -> u64 {
    let off = (irq as u32) * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.handlers[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Drain the IRQ ring: pop vectors off the ring until it's empty, looking
// up each in the IrqTable and (if a handler is registered) invoking it
// via __call_indirect1 + sending EOI via pic_eoi. Re-declared byte-
// identically to irq.vuma:461. The externs (__call_indirect1, pic_eoi)
// take only u64/u8 scalars — neither IrqRing nor IrqTable is passed as
// an extern arg, so neither State is invalidated across the calls.
fn irq_dispatch_loop(ring: State<IrqRing>, tbl: State<IrqTable>) {
    while true {
        let v = irq_ring_pop(ring);
        if v == 9999 {
            return;  // ring empty — drained
        }
        let irq = v as u8;
        let handler = irq_get_handler(tbl, irq);
        if handler != 0 {
            let handler_ret = __call_indirect1(handler, irq as u64);
            pic_eoi(irq);
        }
        // Spurious IRQ (handler == 0): skip EOI (K11 will add spurious EOI).
    }
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Trap dispatcher.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Routes by `tf.vector`. W13S1: the signature now carries the per-CPU
// SyscallTable + IrqRing + IrqTable so trap_syscall / trap_irq can reach
// them without globals (VUMA has no global mutable state — all shared
// state is threaded through State<T> params). kmain will allocate these
// once at boot and pass them down the trap_entry → trap_handler call
// chain. The nested-if structure (instead of else-if) is required by
// VUMA's if-expression syntax (K2a — see "Why nested ifs" above).
//
// The dispatcher reads ONLY tf.vector in the router itself. The sub-
// handlers may read other fields (tf.pc, tf.sp, tf.error_code, etc.) but
// all such reads are through the superset field names — NO arch-specific
// field names like tf.rax / tf.x0 / tf.ra appear anywhere. This is the
// R2 Golden Rule in TASKS.md ("Do not write arch-conditional logic
// inside kernel transforms").

// Trap dispatcher: routes by vector number.
//   vector 0..31   → CPU exception → trap_panic
//   vector 128     → syscall       → trap_syscall
//   everything else → IRQ          → trap_irq
// W13S1: syscall_tbl / irq_ring / irq_tbl threaded through so the
// sub-handlers can reach the per-CPU dispatch state.
fn trap_handler(tf: State<TrapFrame>, syscall_tbl: State<SyscallTable>, irq_ring: State<IrqRing>, irq_tbl: State<IrqTable>) {
    let v = tf.vector;
    if v < 32 {
        // CPU exception — panic path.
        trap_panic(tf);
    } else {
        if v == 128 {
            // Syscall (int 0x80 on x86_64 / SVC #0 on aarch64 / ECALL on
            // riscv64 — normalized to vector == 128 by the per-arch
            // adapter).
            trap_syscall(tf, syscall_tbl);
        } else {
            // Hardware IRQ.
            trap_irq(tf, irq_ring, irq_tbl);
        }
    }
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Per-vector-class sub-handlers (W13S1: de-stubbed).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// CPU exception handler — W13S1: prints the panic banner + vector + PC,
// then halts via exit(1). Previously a bare `return;` stub.
//
// The vector and PC are read from tf into LOCAL u64's (vec_val / pc_val)
// BEFORE the first extern write call — the codegen conservatively
// invalidates State<T> on extern calls, so caching the values first means
// the post-extern invalidation of tf is harmless (same pattern as
// dispatch.vuma's `let nr = tf_get_gpr(tf, 0);` before the indirect call).
//
// The banner + labels use Wave 1 string literals (lowered to .rodata
// addresses; `write` takes them directly as `buf: Address`). The decimal
// expansions of vec_val / pc_val go through a local State<PanicBuf> +
// trap_put_dec + write(1, buf as Address, n) — same PMT "Borrowed read"
// pattern as shell.vuma's `write(1, out as Address, ...)`. The buf is
// reused for the second number (trap_put_dec overwrites buf.data[0..n]
// — a fresh write, safe after the prior extern call).
//
// The self-test does NOT call trap_panic (vector=0 would exit(1) and
// abort the test). The IVE verifier still statically checks this body.
fn trap_panic(tf: State<TrapFrame>) {
    // Cache tf.vector + tf.pc into locals BEFORE the first extern call.
    let vec_val: u64 = tf.vector;
    let pc_val: u64 = tf.pc;

    // Banner.
    write(1, "*** KERNEL PANIC ***\n", 21);

    // Vector.
    write(1, "vector=", 7);
    let buf = state_new(PanicBuf);
    let n1 = trap_put_dec(buf, vec_val);
    write(1, buf as Address, n1 as i64);

    // PC.
    write(1, " pc=", 4);
    let n2 = trap_put_dec(buf, pc_val);
    write(1, buf as Address, n2 as i64);

    // Trailing newline.
    write(1, "\n", 1);

    // Halt.
    exit(1);
    return;  // unreachable — exit does not return
}

// Syscall handler — W13S1: calls syscall_dispatch_from_trap(tbl, tf)
// (re-declared byte-identically to dispatch.vuma R4c above). The dispatch
// reads nr from canonical slot 0, looks up the handler in tbl, invokes
// it via __call_indirect1 if registered (writes result to slot 7), or
// writes 38 (-ENOSYS) to slot 7 if out-of-range / unregistered.
// Previously a bare `return;` stub.
fn trap_syscall(tf: State<TrapFrame>, tbl: State<SyscallTable>) {
    syscall_dispatch_from_trap(tbl, tf);
}

// IRQ handler — W13S1: pushes tf.vector onto the per-CPU IrqRing, then
// drains the ring via irq_dispatch_loop (both re-declared byte-identically
// to irq.vuma R2f above). The push is the producer side of the SPSC ring;
// the drain is the consumer side — pops each vector, looks up the handler
// in tbl, invokes + EOIs if registered, skips if spurious. Previously a
// bare `return;` stub.
fn trap_irq(tf: State<TrapFrame>, ring: State<IrqRing>, tbl: State<IrqTable>) {
    irq_ring_push(ring, tf.vector);
    irq_dispatch_loop(ring, tbl);
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Self-test.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Allocate a TrapFrame + SyscallTable + IrqRing + IrqTable via state_new,
// zero-init the TrapFrame via tf_init, then exercise the syscall + IRQ
// dispatcher branches:
//   (1) vector 128 → trap_syscall (syscall path — empty table → dispatch
//       writes 38 to slot 7, returns 38; no crash).
//   (2) vector 32  → trap_irq (IRQ path — push 32 onto the ring, drain
//       via irq_dispatch_loop; spurious (handler == 0), no EOI; no crash).
//
// The panic path (vector 0 → trap_panic → exit(1)) is NOT exercised —
// exit(1) would abort the test process before the remaining checks run.
// Same pass-path-only pattern as panic.vuma's self-test (assert(1, msg)
// — cond=1 → no-op, panic NOT called). The IVE verifier still statically
// checks trap_panic's body for PMT purity + memory safety.
//
// Per the R2f contract's "Use decimal constants" rule: all non-zero
// constants in the self-test use DECIMAL (32, 128).

fn main() -> i32 {
    let tf = state_new(TrapFrame);
    let syscall_tbl = state_new(SyscallTable);
    let irq_ring = state_new(IrqRing);
    let irq_tbl = state_new(IrqTable);
    tf_init(tf);
    // state_new zero-fills, so syscall_tbl.count / irq_ring.count /
    // irq_ring.head / irq_ring.tail / irq_tbl.count are all 0, and the
    // handlers / buf arrays are all zero bytes. The empty-table paths
    // are what the self-test exercises (no registered handlers).

    // Test 1: vector 128 (syscall) → trap_syscall (no crash).
    // syscall_dispatch_from_trap reads nr from slot 0 (= 0 from tf_init),
    // 0 < 512 so bounds-check passes, looks up handler for syscall 0 in
    // the empty table → handler == 0 → writes 38 to slot 7, returns 38.
    tf.vector = 128;
    trap_handler(tf, syscall_tbl, irq_ring, irq_tbl);

    // Test 2: vector 32 (IRQ) → trap_irq (no crash).
    // irq_ring_push(ring, 32) enqueues 32 (ring.count 0→1). Then
    // irq_dispatch_loop pops 32, looks up handler for IRQ 32 in the
    // empty table → handler == 0 (spurious) → skip EOI, pop again →
    // 9999 (empty) → return. ring.count back to 0.
    tf.vector = 32;
    trap_handler(tf, syscall_tbl, irq_ring, irq_tbl);

    return 0;
}
